SQLite Is Public Domain

All of the code and documentation in SQLite has been dedicated to the public
domain by the authors. All code authors, and representatives of the companies
they work for, have signed affidavits dedicating their contributions to the
public domain and originals of those signed affidavits are stored in a firesafe
at the main offices of Hwaci. All contributors are listed in the SQLite
AUTHORS file in the canonical source tree.

Anyone is free to copy, modify, publish, use, compile, sell, or distribute the
original SQLite code, either in source code form or as a compiled binary, for
any purpose, commercial or non-commercial, and by any means.

The previous paragraph applies to the work of the original authors. SQLite
contains additional code written by other people, who may or may not have
dedicated their work to the public domain. Where this is known, the source
files note that fact. Where it is not known, the SQLite project treats the
code as public domain.

In lieu of a legal notice, here is a blessing:

    May you do good and not evil.
    May you find forgiveness for yourself and forgive others.
    May you share freely, never taking more than you give.

This tree vendors the official amalgamation of SQLite 3.53.4
(`sqlite-amalgamation-3530400`) from https://www.sqlite.org/download.html
(https://www.sqlite.org/2026/sqlite-amalgamation-3530400.zip).
